    "updated_at": "2026-11-12T17:03:18Z",
    "body": "Rework (UMA) @ 81e506d2 - merged `upstream/main` (no rebase, no force-push) and re-derived the standing half on #818's API. PR stays a draft; it needs a re-check.\n\n**Conflict (`posting_index_swing.rs`).** I took main's version whole: `Verdict`/`verdict`, `Nose`, `index_angle(path, line)`, the `distance` about a line or the origin, and stacked rotaries. On top of it I only made `Verdict`/`Nose`/`verdict`/`index_angle` `pub(super)` and changed `swept_radius` into `swept(..) -> Swept { r, span }`. `span` is the work's extent along the line, `(p - o) . u`. The tool half reads `.r` and works exactly as before, so no #804/#818 verdict moved. `posting_swing_standing.rs` now gates on `verdict()` (`Verdict::Judged { letter, line, stacked, .. }`) instead of the removed `work_a`.\n\n**Findings:**\n1. *Conflict with main.* Resolved as above, keeping both laws.\n2. *Y-line, other level lines, stacked rotaries.* Each one is now judged or named, never skipped in silence:\n   - **One level line `u`** (A about X, B about Y, or a stated level vector): a fixed fixture is beside the work when its span along `u` misses the work's span. Otherwise its distance is taken from its `(s, z)` box, with `s` along `u x Z`. On X and Y that is exact for a box. Off both axes it is a lower bound, so it can refuse more but never clears falsely. The face check uses that rotary's `axis_to_table`. A box that reaches across the line is named not judged.\n   - **Stacked rotaries:** a fixed fixture is judged by its box distance from the WCS origin, against the sweep about the origin (the same sphere the tool half uses). A box that holds the origin is named not judged. The face is **named not judged** (\"turns the work on more than one rotary: the face under them moves...\"), because one `axis_to_table` cannot say which face stands still.\n   - Pins in `index_swing_horizontal`:\n     - `a_table_b_judges_the_work_against_standing_steel_about_y`: face hit and clear. A block beside the work along Y is clear. A riser past the bar's end in X is struck; the old about-X law would have cleared it.\n     - `an_a_stated_level_but_off_x_judges_standing_steel_about_its_own_line`: face hit; a plate clear at margin + 1; a riser hit 2 mm inside the margin.\n     - `stacked_rotaries_judge_standing_steel_from_the_wcs_origin_and_name_the_face`: the face is named; a far fixture clears; a block beside along B but inside the sphere is struck.\n3. *Gate re-run on the merged head.* Done, see below.\n- Non-blocking: I pinned the `TOUCH_MM` end-face edge with a \"touching\" block in `a_standing_fixture_under_the_work_is_judged_by_its_distance_from_the_line`. I also updated the decision file with the re-derivation and the dropped alternative (refusing stacked machines outright). I did not take up the `posting.rs` size note or the machine-docs line.\n\n**Gate on 81e506d2:** `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main` passed: post suites, then 133 app suites plus `--lib`, including `index_swing` (13), `index_swing_horizontal` (16), `microarc_test_part`, `fourth_axis_pose`, `tormach_fourth_axis`, `rotary_verify` and `post_not_judged`. Wall 4.2 min, 0 failed. `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-post --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean.\n\nChangelog line unchanged; body `Refs #1107` on the merge commit, PR still `Fixes #1107`.\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
